@@ -137,6 +137,67 @@ to indicates which is the bigger version:
 * ``1`` if the first version is greater than the second version.
 
 
+pysemver sort
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Sort versions, oldest first.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver sort [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    The versions to sort. If none is given, versions are read from
+    *standard in*, one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The sorted versions are printed on *standard out* as plain text, one
+version per line, so the output can be read line by line, for example
+with ``while read v``::
+
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+
+If no version is passed and stdin is empty, nothing is printed. If any version is invalid,
+nothing is printed on standard out; an error message is printed on
+standard error and the return code is 2.
+
+
+Versions can also be piped in::
+
+    $ printf '1.10.0\n\n1.2.0\n' | pysemver sort
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+
+
+pysemver latest
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Print the highest version that is not a prerelease.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver latest [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    The versions to check. If none is given, versions are read from
+    *standard in*, one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The result is printed as JSON on *standard out*: the version as a string,
+or ``null`` if there is no version that is not a prerelease::
+
+    $ pysemver latest 1.2.0 2.0.0-rc.1 1.10.0
+    "1.10.0"
+    $ pysemver latest 2.0.0-rc.1
+    null
+
+If any version is invalid, nothing is printed on standard out and the
+return code is 2.
+
+
 Return Code
 -----------
 
